{"toolCallId":"call_f2fb3960b6b24d62889a5099de45af30","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w776a5f2639/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"[2-3 sentences: what this implements and the technical approach]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.4 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n<!-- FILL: Fill the stack row and write three sentences: what changes, where, and how you will prove it. GOOD: Change one bash script. Add one `--keep <n>` option. Prove it with an archive count. WEAK: This plan describes the implementation. -->\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., TypeScript, Python 3.11] |\n| **Framework** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., React, FastAPI] |\n| **Storage** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., PostgreSQL, None] |\n| **Testing** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: e.g., Jest, pytest] |\n\n### Overview\n[2-3 sentences: what this implements and the technical approach]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n<!-- FILL: Turn each gate into a check a command can answer. Keep the boxes unticked until they pass. GOOD: `ls scripts/rotate-logs.sh` prints the path and `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 10 before the task starts. WEAK: The team agrees the plan is ready. -->\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [ ] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [ ] Success criteria measurable\n- [ ] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: The test command and the output it must print]\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n<!-- FILL: Name the pattern in three words, then list the one component that changes. GOOD: Single script branch. The component is `scripts/rotate-logs.sh`. WEAK: Standard architecture. -->\n\n### Pattern\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: MVC | MVVM | Clean Architecture | Serverless | Monolith | Other]\n\n### Key Components\n- **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Component 1]**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Purpose]\n- **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Component 2]**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Purpose]\n\n### Data Flow\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description of how data moves through the system]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n<!-- FILL: Point to `tasks.md` and name the three phases in one line. GOOD: Setup counts the archives. Implementation adds `--keep`. Verification counts 3. WEAK: See tasks. -->\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n<!-- FILL: Name the one command you will run and the output it must print. GOOD: `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 3 after `--keep 3` and 10 after a plain run. WEAK: Test it manually. -->\n\nN/A — record any testing beyond the verification tasks in `tasks.md` here.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: List each tool the change needs, or write `None`. GOOD: `bash` and `awk`. No new package. WEAK: Usual dependencies. -->\n\nN/A — record dependencies beyond the components named in the architecture here.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n<!-- FILL: Name the trigger and the one command that reverts the change. GOOD: Trigger: `--keep 3` removes an archive newer than the three kept. Revert the one commit. WEAK: Roll back if needed. -->\n\nN/A — record rollback steps beyond reverting the scoped change here.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"Add a --min-percent <N> case to the option loop of calculate-completeness.sh, keep the parsed value in a new global, and compare it against the percentage the run already computed after the report is written. The report path is untouched, so stdout stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.4 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash, written to Bash 3.2 compatibility like the rest of `runtime/cli/spec/` |\n| **Framework** | None - a single standalone script |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | Manual CLI invocations against a fixture spec folder, asserting the exit status and diffing stdout |\n\n### Overview\nChange one file, `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh`. Add a `--min-percent <N>` case to the existing `while`/`case` option loop, store the value in a new `MIN_PERCENT` global, and after `output_text` or `output_json` has run, compare the percentage the run computed against `MIN_PERCENT` and exit `3` when it is lower. Because the comparison sits after the output call and never touches the output functions, the report is byte-identical with and without the flag, and the check is proved by diffing two runs and reading their exit statuses.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 80 specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/scratch/fixture-67` exits `3`, and the same command with `--min-percent 50` exits `0`\n- [ ] `diff <(run <folder>) <(run --min-percent 50 <folder>)` prints nothing\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle script, one added branch.\n\n### Key Components\n- **Option loop (`§8 MAIN SCRIPT`)**: the `while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]` / `case \"$1\"` block gains a `--min-percent)` arm that consumes two arguments and validates the second.\n- **`MIN_PERCENT` global (`§3 GLOBALS`)**: holds the parsed threshold, defaulting to an empty string so \"no flag\" is distinguishable from `0`.\n- **Post-output comparison (`§8 MAIN SCRIPT`)**: reads the same percentage the report used - `OVERALL_PERCENTAGE` in folder mode, the sourced `percentage` in `--file` mode - and maps it to an exit status.\n- **`show_help` (`§4 HELPER FUNCTIONS`)**: gains one `OPTIONS:` line so the flag is discoverable.\n\n### Data Flow\nArguments are parsed left to right. `--min-percent` takes the next token as its value, validates it as an integer in `0..100`, and stores it in `MIN_PERCENT`; an invalid value prints `ERROR:` on stderr and exits `1` before any report is produced. `calculate_spec_completeness` or `calculate_file_completeness` then computes the percentage as it does today. `output_json` or `output_text` writes the report exactly as it does today. Only after that does the main body compare the computed percentage with `MIN_PERCENT` and choose the exit status: `3` when below, `0` otherwise.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n\nSetup confirms the script rejects the flag today and builds a 67% fixture folder. Implementation adds the option arm, the `MIN_PERCENT` global and the post-output comparison, plus the help and footer lines. Verification runs the below-threshold, at-threshold, above-threshold, unchanged-output and invalid-value cases.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nTesting is manual CLI work against one fixture, because the script has no test suite of its own and this packet does not add one.\n\nBuild the fixture under `scratch/fixture-67/`: a single `spec.md` holding 2 placeholder lines and 4 content lines, which the measured behavior block in `spec.md` records as `Overall Completion: 67%`.\n\n| Case | Command | Expected |\n|------|---------|----------|\n| Below threshold | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 80 <fixture>` | exit `3`, report still on stdout |\n| At threshold | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 67 <fixture>` | exit `0` |\n| Above threshold | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 50 <fixture>` | exit `0` |\n| Unchanged output | `diff <(calculate-completeness.sh <fixture>) <(calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 50 <fixture>)` | no output, both exit `0` |\n| Unchanged JSON | `diff <(calculate-completeness.sh --json <fixture>) <(calculate-completeness.sh --json --min-percent 50 <fixture>)` | no output |\n| No flag | `calculate-completeness.sh <fixture>` | exit `0` |\n| Missing value | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent` | `ERROR:` on stderr, exit `1` |\n| Non-integer | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent abc <fixture>` | `ERROR:` on stderr, exit `1` |\n| Negative | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent -1 <fixture>` | `ERROR:` on stderr, exit `1` |\n| Over 100 | `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 101 <fixture>` | `ERROR:` on stderr, exit `1` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n`bash` and `bc`. Both are already required: the script checks for `bc` at startup and exits `1` when it is missing. No new package, tool or runtime version is introduced.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\nTrigger: any run that printed the report but exited `1`, or any diff between a plain run and a `--min-percent` run that is not empty.\n\nRevert the one commit that touches `calculate-completeness.sh`. The change is additive - a new `case` arm, one new global, one comparison block and two documentation lines - so `git checkout -- <script>` restores the prior behavior with no data migration and no follow-up step.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":39,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":25}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w776a5f2639/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_faddf75dc6654587930cbc1aad32e178","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w776a5f2639/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent\"\n  - \"add a min percent option to calculate completeness\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.4 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n<!-- FILL: Fill every row: level, priority, status, date and branch. Use the real branch name. GOOD: Priority P1. Status Draft. Branch `feat/rotate-logs-keep`. WEAK: Priority TBD. Status maybe. -->\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 1 |\n| **Priority** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-completeness-min-percent` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n<!-- FILL: State the problem in two sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The rotate-logs script never deletes old archives, so a host that copies `logs/archive` fills its disk. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:measured-behavior -->\n### Measured Behavior\n<!-- FILL: Run the command this change affects and paste the command and its verbatim output. Quote every number the spec states about current behavior from this block. Write `N/A:` and a reason when no command applies. GOOD: `bash scripts/rotate-logs.sh` with 10 archives, then `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 10. WEAK: The script deletes old logs. -->\n```text\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: command and its verbatim output]\n```\n<!-- /ANCHOR:measured-behavior -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--keep <n>` option that deletes all but the newest n archives in `logs/archive/`. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With 10 archives, `bash scripts/rotate-logs.sh --keep 3` exits 0 and `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 3. WEAK: The option should work well. -->\nREQ ids run in one sequence across the P0 and P1 tables. A new requirement takes the highest REQ id plus one. validate.sh does not link ids between files.\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How to verify it's done] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How to verify it's done] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: With 10 archives in `logs/archive`, `bash scripts/rotate-logs.sh --keep 3` leaves 3. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A script that reads `--keep` with no value could delete every archive. Mitigation: reject a missing value with exit 2 and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 7. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does a missing `--keep` value delete every archive? Decision: no, a missing value exits 2. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\"\ndescription: \"calculate-completeness.sh prints a completion percentage but always exits 0, so a caller that wants to fail below a threshold has to parse stdout. Adding --min-percent <N> gives callers a machine-readable floor via exit code 3 while leaving the report byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"completeness min percent\"\n  - \"add a min percent option to calculate completeness\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core | v2.4 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a min-percent option to calculate-completeness.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 1 -->\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 1 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-completeness-min-percent` (not created; the packet was scaffolded with `--skip-branch`, so the working tree is on a detached `HEAD`) |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`calculate-completeness.sh` computes and prints a completion percentage, then ends with an unconditional `exit 0`. A caller that wants to fail when a spec folder is less complete than a required floor must scrape the `Overall Completion:` line out of stdout and compare it itself. Any change to the report formatting breaks every such caller, and a shell pipeline cannot gate on the result with a plain `if`.\n\n### Purpose\nLet a caller state its completion floor as one flag and read the answer from the exit status, with the existing report left exactly as it is.\n\n### Measured Behavior\n<!-- FILL: Run the command this change affects and paste the command and its verbatim output. Quote every number the spec states about current behavior from this block. Write `N/A:` and a reason when no command applies. GOOD: `bash scripts/rotate-logs.sh` with 10 archives, then `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 10. WEAK: The script deletes old logs. -->\n```text\n$ bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent\n\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         SpecKit Completeness Calculator - Results\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nSpec Folder: specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent\nFiles Analyzed: 3\n\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nOverall Completion: 100%\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nTotal Placeholders: 0\nTotal Content Lines: 164\n\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nStatus: ✅ Excellent - Nearly complete\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n$ echo \"EXIT=$?\"\nEXIT=0\n\n$ bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 50 specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent\nERROR: Unexpected argument: 50\nUse --help for usage information\n$ echo \"EXIT=$?\"\nEXIT=1\n```\n\nThe second run shows the option does not exist today: `--min-percent` is consumed as the positional `SPEC_FOLDER`, so the value `50` lands in the catch-all branch and the run dies with exit 1.\n\nA folder that is genuinely partial reports a fractional figure. A fixture holding one `spec.md` with 2 placeholders over 6 content lines prints `Overall Completion: 67%` and still exits 0, which is the case the new flag has to fail.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:measured-behavior -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add a `--min-percent <N>` option to the option-parsing loop of `calculate-completeness.sh`.\n- Compare the percentage the run computed against `N` and exit `3` when it is below `N`, `0` otherwise.\n- Reject a missing, non-integer, negative, or greater-than-100 value for `N` with the script's existing `ERROR:` form on stderr and exit `1`.\n- List the new option in `show_help` and add exit code `3` to the script's exit-code footer.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- Any change to the printed report or the JSON payload - the feature requires the output to stay unchanged, so the status labels, the `Overall Completion:` line and the JSON keys are all frozen.\n- Any change to how the percentage is calculated - placeholder counting, line counting and the `bc` rounding stay as they are.\n- An environment variable or config-file form of the threshold - the request names one CLI flag.\n- Wiring the new exit code into `validate.sh` or any other caller - no caller is named, and changing one is a separate packet.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh` | Modify | Add `--min-percent` parsing, the threshold comparison, one help line and one exit-code footer line |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\nREQ ids run in one sequence across the P0 and P1 tables. A new requirement takes the highest REQ id plus one. validate.sh does not link ids between files.\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-001 | With `--min-percent <N>`, a folder run whose `Overall Completion` is below `N` exits `3`. | On the 67% fixture, `calculate-completeness.sh --min-percent 80 <fixture>` exits `3`. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--min-percent <N>`, a folder run whose `Overall Completion` is at or above `N` exits `0`. | On the 67% fixture, both `--min-percent 67` and `--min-percent 50` exit `0`. |\n| REQ-003 | The report is unchanged by the option. | `diff <(run <folder>) <(run --min-percent 50 <folder>)` prints nothing, and the JSON payload from `--json` is identical with and without the option. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement | Acceptance Criteria |\n|----|-------------|---------------------|\n| REQ-004 | An unusable `N` is rejected before any report is printed, with an `ERROR:` line on stderr and exit `1`. `N` must be an integer from 0 to 100. | `--min-percent` with no value, `--min-percent abc`, `--min-percent -1` and `--min-percent 101` each print an `ERROR:` line on stderr and exit `1`. |\n| REQ-005 | `show_help` lists the new option. | `calculate-completeness.sh --help` prints a line containing `--min-percent <N>`. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: On the 67% fixture, `--min-percent 80` exits `3` and prints the unchanged report on stdout.\n- **SC-002**: On the same fixture, `--min-percent 50` exits `0`.\n- **SC-003**: stdout of a plain run and of a `--min-percent 50` run against the same folder are byte-identical, in both text and `--json` modes.\n- **SC-004**: `--min-percent` with no value exits `1` and writes nothing to stdout.\n- **SC-005**: A plain run with no `--min-percent` still exits `0` at any percentage.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bc`, already required by the script and checked at startup | None - no new dependency is introduced | Nothing to do; the check at startup already fails fast when `bc` is missing |\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2, which the script is written against | A newer-only construct would break the macOS default shell | Use only `[[ ]]` integer tests and plain arithmetic, as the rest of the script does |\n| Risk | `set -euo pipefail` aborts the script when a bare `[[ ... ]]` comparison is the last command of a function and evaluates false | The report would never print and the caller would see exit 1 instead of 3 or 0 | Compute the exit status in the main body after output is written, in an explicit `if`/`else` that always ends on a zero-status statement |\n| Risk | Exit `3` already means \"system error\" elsewhere in `spec/*.sh`, so a caller cannot tell a threshold miss from a broken run by the number alone | A wrapper could misreport a below-threshold result as a tooling failure | The feature fixes `3` as the below-threshold code, so document it in the footer and in `--help`; the report on stdout still names the percentage |\n| Risk | `--min-percent` is consumed as the positional `SPEC_FOLDER` if it is added anywhere but the option loop | The flag silently becomes a path and the run reports \"Spec folder not found\" | Add the case to the loop before the `*)` catch-all and cover it with the T001 before/after check |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 7. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does the option apply in `--file` single-file mode, where the report line is `Completion:` rather than `Overall Completion:`? Decision: yes, compare against the percentage that run reports, so the flag is never silently ignored. REQ-001 and REQ-002 name the folder label because that is the term the request used.\n- Does \"output unchanged\" forbid touching `--help`? Decision: no. It scopes to the results report, and REQ-003 pins that. `--help` gains one option line under REQ-005.\n- Should a value equal to the reported percentage pass? Decision: yes. The request says \"below N\" fails, so `N` itself passes.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":63,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":31}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w776a5f2639/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-completeness-min-percent/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
